1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,240
 Hi, in this lesson, I will talk about workspace indexing in copilot inside VS Code.

2
00:00:06,240 --> 00:00:11,520
 Let's get started. When you ask a pilot a question, it doesn't just guess.

3
00:00:11,520 --> 00:00:17,280
 It actually looks into your project to understand how files connect,

4
00:00:17,280 --> 00:00:20,720
 where functions are defined and how components work together.

5
00:00:20,720 --> 00:00:27,520
 And that's called workspace context. It's what help copilers give answers that match your

6
00:00:27,520 --> 00:00:33,440
 own code, not random examples from the internet. When you open a project, VS Code

7
00:00:33,440 --> 00:00:39,360
 quietly scans it in the background. It looks through all your files, your folder structure,

8
00:00:39,360 --> 00:00:46,080
 and your code symbols such as classes and functions. It also notices which file your editing

9
00:00:46,080 --> 00:00:51,040
 and what text you have selected. That helps it focus on what's most relevant.

10
00:00:52,560 --> 00:00:57,280
 All this information is stored in something called a workspace index.

11
00:00:57,280 --> 00:01:03,360
 Link of it as a smart searchable map of your project. This is what copilot uses to quickly

12
00:01:03,360 --> 00:01:07,840
 find and understand the codes that relates to your question. For small projects,

13
00:01:07,840 --> 00:01:13,280
 copilot can include your entire workspace directly when it answers your question.

14
00:01:13,280 --> 00:01:20,960
 For larger projects, it only keeps the most relevant parts, so everything stays fast and focused.

15
00:01:20,960 --> 00:01:25,040
 VS Code can use different search methods depending on what's available,

16
00:01:25,040 --> 00:01:28,960
 and sometimes it searches by meaning using semantic search.

17
00:01:28,960 --> 00:01:36,240
 Sometimes it looks by file name or exact text, and if your project is on GitHub,

18
00:01:36,240 --> 00:01:40,240
 it can even use GitHub's code search to find results more quickly.

19
00:01:40,240 --> 00:01:46,240
 It combines all these methods to pick the best results before giving you an answer.

20
00:01:46,240 --> 00:01:49,760
 The workspace index is the key parts that powers all of this.

21
00:01:50,640 --> 00:01:54,640
 It's what copilot uses to understand and search your code base quickly.

22
00:01:54,640 --> 00:02:01,280
 Depending on your setup, the index can leave remotely on GitHub or Azure DevOps,

23
00:02:01,280 --> 00:02:06,640
 or locally on your own computer. If your code is stored on GitHub or Azure DevOps,

24
00:02:06,640 --> 00:02:13,360
 VS Code uses a remote index. This index is built automatically the first time you use

25
00:02:13,360 --> 00:02:20,640
 out workspace or zodiac code base in chat. After that, it updates automatically every time you push

26
00:02:20,640 --> 00:02:28,080
 new code. It's fast and perfect for large repositories, and you don't have to do anything manually.

27
00:02:28,080 --> 00:02:34,160
 If your project isn't on GitHub or Azure DevOps, VS Code creates a local index instead.

28
00:02:34,160 --> 00:02:41,120
 This index is saved on your own machine and used only for faster searching inside your project.

29
00:02:41,920 --> 00:02:48,560
 For small projects, it's built automatically. For medium sized ones, you can open the command

30
00:02:48,560 --> 00:02:55,600
 palette and run build local workspace index once, and if your project is very large,

31
00:02:55,600 --> 00:03:05,920
 more than about 2500 files. VS Code will switch to simpler basic index. The basic index is a

32
00:03:05,920 --> 00:03:13,200
 lightweight version that works when your project is too large for a local index. It doesn't include

33
00:03:13,200 --> 00:03:19,920
 all the deep semantic search features, but it still helps co-pilot find relevant code quickly.

34
00:03:19,920 --> 00:03:26,800
 For most projects, it's more than enough to get good answers in no time. You can always check

35
00:03:26,800 --> 00:03:32,800
 what type of index co-pilot is using from the status bar at the bottom of VS Code. It shows

36
00:03:32,800 --> 00:03:38,240
 whether the index is being built or updated and whether it's remote, local, or basic.

37
00:03:38,240 --> 00:03:42,800
 If you need to, you can also rebuild it manually from the command palette.

38
00:03:42,800 --> 00:03:52,080
 Just find commands like build local workspace index, build remote workspace index, VS Code indexes,

39
00:03:52,080 --> 00:03:58,320
 all text-based files that belong to your project that includes your code, configuration files,

40
00:03:58,320 --> 00:04:04,320
 and documentation. It skips things like images, PDFs, and temporary files.

41
00:04:04,320 --> 00:04:11,680
 Files listed in.gittignore or excluded in VS Code settings are also skipped.

42
00:04:11,680 --> 00:04:18,320
 But if you open one of those ignored files, co-pilot will still read it for the session to

43
00:04:18,320 --> 00:04:24,640
 make sure your question makes sense. To sum it up, workspace indexing is what help co-pilot

44
00:04:24,640 --> 00:04:29,280
 understand your entire code base, not just the file you're currently working on.

45
00:04:29,280 --> 00:04:34,880
 It happens automatically and adjusts based on your project size and where your code lives.

46
00:04:34,880 --> 00:04:40,000
 You can always check or rebuild it from the status bar or the command menu.

47
00:04:40,000 --> 00:04:48,000
 Once you understand how indexing works, you'll see why co-pilot can give such smart,

48
00:04:48,000 --> 00:04:55,280
 context where answers about your project. It's not guessing anymore, it's actually reading your code.

49
00:04:55,280 --> 00:05:01,120
 I hope this lesson was useful for you and see you in the next lesson.

